WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.510 --> 00:00:04.260
Pixar...the studio behind this,
and this, and this

00:00:04.260 --> 00:00:07.620
has mastered a sort of
photo-surrealistic style

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.930
where there’s a sense of cartoony-ness

00:00:09.930 --> 00:00:14.120
but most things look and feel like they
could exist in the real world.

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:16.615
Like these fibers on Mr. Incredible’s shirt.

00:00:16.615 --> 00:00:19.602
And this tree stump and grass from
this scene from Up.

00:00:19.602 --> 00:00:23.498
Or the glimmering, out-of-focus bokeh
behind Bo Peep.

00:00:23.498 --> 00:00:25.950
And it’s not just Pixar.

00:00:25.950 --> 00:00:27.740
These movies look so similar

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:31.485
you can barely tell they’re from
different animation studios.

00:00:31.485 --> 00:00:33.518
But when you look at these movies

00:00:33.518 --> 00:00:36.771
it’s clear that something different
is happening.

00:00:36.771 --> 00:00:40.000
These lights don’t just illuminate;
they reveal a texture.

00:00:40.000 --> 00:00:42.399
This tree in the background 
isn’t out of focus

00:00:42.399 --> 00:00:44.501
it’s just got a simplified look.

00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:48.093
And this hair looks more like
brush strokes than human hair.

00:00:48.093 --> 00:00:50.210
These films are part of a new trend

00:00:50.210 --> 00:00:53.415
that’s steering animation away from 
replicating the real world

00:00:53.415 --> 00:00:55.109
and into somewhere new.

00:00:58.019 --> 00:01:01.249
Crafting beautiful animation in CG films

00:01:01.249 --> 00:01:05.185
requires a lot of dedicated artists
to perform specialized tasks:

00:01:05.185 --> 00:01:06.342
like rendering.

00:01:06.342 --> 00:01:09.096
Rendering is the process by which
a computer takes

00:01:09.096 --> 00:01:14.220
geometry, textures, lighting, camera inputs 
and does a bunch of math

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:17.795
to apply all that information into
a final image.

00:01:17.795 --> 00:01:19.266
Like this one.

00:01:19.266 --> 00:01:23.470
Most render engines, like the one I’m using
here, are physically based

00:01:23.470 --> 00:01:25.120
which essentially means that

00:01:25.120 --> 00:01:30.470
it tries to replicate the real world physics
of light, shadow, reflection, and more.

00:01:30.470 --> 00:01:33.550
You can see this type of rendering
all over Pixar’s work

00:01:33.550 --> 00:01:35.873
even going back to their first movie.

00:01:35.873 --> 00:01:39.224
Rex looks like he could exist 
in the real world.

00:01:39.224 --> 00:01:42.410
Every meeting we would have, everybody
brought up like the Pixar look.

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:45.480
The Pixar look was something that was very
high quality

00:01:45.480 --> 00:01:47.230
very successful at the box office

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:49.470
so everybody kind of wanted the Pixar look.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:51.409
My name is Christos Obrenetov.

00:01:51.409 --> 00:01:54.630
Christos is the CEO of Lollipop Shaders

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:58.715
a company that builds custom shaders
and plugins for 3D software.

00:01:58.715 --> 00:01:59.970
He’s worked on tons of films

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:04.755
including "Life of Pi", "A Christmas Carol,"
and the upcoming "Across the Spider-Verse".

00:02:04.755 --> 00:02:05.930
But before all that

00:02:05.930 --> 00:02:09.853
he worked on a bunch of films that tried to
replicate that Pixar look.

00:02:09.853 --> 00:02:13.035
Because it was reliable, popular, and safe.

00:02:13.035 --> 00:02:16.680
Part of doing a feature film is, you know,
a lot of money and a lot of time.

00:02:16.680 --> 00:02:20.369
So they were kind of chasing that Pixar dream
of having that kind of a success.

00:02:20.369 --> 00:02:23.239
So to go and spend, you know, $100 million
or more...

00:02:23.239 --> 00:02:26.264
the investors and what not
are probably saying:

00:02:26.264 --> 00:02:28.690
"It's got to look like Pixar or Disney."
Right?

00:02:28.690 --> 00:02:31.447
And that was the case for a long time.

00:02:31.447 --> 00:02:34.391
Even when studios wanted to try
something different:

00:02:34.391 --> 00:02:38.049
Disney would come up and interview for a movie
that ended up being called "Bolt".

00:02:38.049 --> 00:02:39.989
They were showing us this really cool concept
art and they're like,

00:02:39.989 --> 00:02:41.150
"Oh, it's going to be super stylized."

00:02:41.150 --> 00:02:43.079
"It's going to look like sort of like 
the concept art."

00:02:43.079 --> 00:02:44.079
And it was very exciting.

00:02:44.079 --> 00:02:45.349
I was like, "Oh, this is great."

00:02:45.349 --> 00:02:46.972
And then when the movie 
did come out

00:02:46.972 --> 00:02:51.419
I mean, Bolt is fantastic, but they made it
very, you know, that same look.

00:02:51.419 --> 00:02:56.049
Movies like "Monster House" and "A Christmas
Carol" were intended to look stylized too

00:02:56.049 --> 00:02:59.439
but ultimately they settled back into
that tried-and-true

00:02:59.439 --> 00:03:01.507
physically based rendering look.

00:03:01.507 --> 00:03:05.050
There were glimmers of more stylized approaches
in short film tests

00:03:05.050 --> 00:03:07.742
and small sequences like in Disney’s Moana.

00:03:07.742 --> 00:03:11.590
But this stylized sequence
is only about 30 seconds

00:03:11.590 --> 00:03:14.249
out of the hour and 43 minute-long film.

00:03:14.249 --> 00:03:16.752
A lot of studios have been playing with
this for a long time.

00:03:16.752 --> 00:03:17.999
But to take that risk

00:03:17.999 --> 00:03:21.231
to do a full feature film that's
very stylized in 3D

00:03:21.231 --> 00:03:23.060
I think people were scared.

00:03:23.060 --> 00:03:26.795
And that fear kept studios from making
bold choices.

00:03:26.795 --> 00:03:30.964
Until 2017 when the trailer for
"Into the Spider-Verse" dropped.

00:03:30.964 --> 00:03:35.226
It had simplified graphic bursts that felt
like comic book panels.

00:03:35.226 --> 00:03:37.559
There was no motion blur or depth of field

00:03:37.559 --> 00:03:42.616
and everything, from the characters to the
environments, was full of texture.

00:03:42.616 --> 00:03:45.439
According to one of the animation supervisors
for the project

00:03:45.439 --> 00:03:47.169
after a year and a half of work

00:03:47.169 --> 00:03:51.221
the studio was still nervous that people were
going to hate the visuals.

00:03:51.221 --> 00:03:54.700
Because they felt like they were taking too
many risks.

00:03:54.700 --> 00:03:57.909
Instead of leaning into the safe, physically
based, rendering look

00:03:57.909 --> 00:04:01.650
"Into the Spider-Verse" chose to use 
non-photorealistic rendering.

00:04:01.650 --> 00:04:05.917
To pull this off, they essentially had to
break their physically-based renderer.

00:04:05.917 --> 00:04:08.973
Instead of inputting data from lights, 
camera, and materials

00:04:08.973 --> 00:04:11.420
and receiving a realistic looking render

00:04:11.420 --> 00:04:15.480
they combined all that data with custom
data passes that tweaked things like

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:18.940
the focus plane or the way the light worked
in an image.

00:04:18.940 --> 00:04:22.644
This combo allowed the renderer 
to produce stylized results.

00:04:22.644 --> 00:04:27.456
For example, in "Into the Spider-verse",
out of focus elements aren’t blurry.

00:04:27.456 --> 00:04:29.030
Instead, the colors split

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:32.743
as if the screen printing on an actual 
comic book was done poorly.

00:04:32.743 --> 00:04:35.384
Lighting and shadow were approached
in new ways too.

00:04:35.384 --> 00:04:37.682
Light often reveals halftone dots.

00:04:37.682 --> 00:04:40.695
And shadows create sketchy hatch marks.

00:04:40.695 --> 00:04:43.982
But there’s more than just shading magic
happening here.

00:04:43.982 --> 00:04:47.054
Non-photorealistic renderings often use linework

00:04:47.054 --> 00:04:49.400
2D elements like speed lines and doubles

00:04:49.400 --> 00:04:53.720
and variable frame rates to pull these frames
further away from reality

00:04:53.720 --> 00:04:57.384
and often, closer to the concept art.

00:04:57.384 --> 00:04:59.450
Just two months after its release

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:03.500
"Into the Spider-Verse" became the highest grossing
film Sony Pictures animation

00:05:03.500 --> 00:05:05.070
had ever made.

00:05:05.070 --> 00:05:08.319
It doesn’t hold a candle to Pixar
in terms of lifetime gross:

00:05:08.319 --> 00:05:10.250
"Incredibles 2" sits at the number one spot

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.700
while Spider-verse doesn’t even crack
the top 50.

00:05:12.700 --> 00:05:16.671
But, much like Pixar did in the first wave
of CG animated features

00:05:16.671 --> 00:05:21.840
"Into the Spider-Verse" redefined
the visual goals for animation studios.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:26.280
2021’s "The Mitchells vs the Machines" and
2022’s "Puss in Boots"

00:05:26.280 --> 00:05:30.660
take non-photorealistic rendering in a different,
more painterly direction

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:33.857
where out-of-focus objects 
become simplified shapes

00:05:33.857 --> 00:05:37.640
emulating the way that artists may use 
simplified marks in backgrounds

00:05:37.640 --> 00:05:42.410
"In the Mitchells vs the Machines", shadows
create different marker textures.

00:05:42.410 --> 00:05:47.810
Puss in Boots’ simplified 2D cutaways mimic
the graphic bursts of Into the Spider-verse.

00:05:47.810 --> 00:05:51.172
People were like, "Oh, this is looks really
good and it's different".

00:05:51.172 --> 00:05:56.165
So now, all future projects at studios from
DreamWorks and Imageworks and Pixar—

00:05:56.165 --> 00:05:59.470
they're looking at the next whatever, five
years or whatnot.

00:05:59.470 --> 00:06:01.674
It's all like very stylized.

00:06:01.674 --> 00:06:03.260
And that’s really exciting

00:06:03.260 --> 00:06:07.480
because non-photorealism allows animated movies
to take advantage of

00:06:07.480 --> 00:06:09.382
the things that make them special.

00:06:09.833 --> 00:06:13.412
They can be anything the imagination allows.

